  "question": {
    "text": "The following sets of quantum numbers represent four electrons in an atom.\n(i) $n=4, l=1$\n(ii) $n=4, l=0$\n(iii) $n=3, l=2$\n(iv) $n=3, l=1$\nThe sequence representing increasing order of energy, is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "qk5s8",
      "text": "\n(iii) < (i) < (iv) < (ii)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bZi7z",
      "text": "\n(iv) < (ii) < (iii) < (i)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Pkw60",
      "text": "\n(i) < (iii) < (ii) < (iv)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "5FYw3",
      "text": "\n(ii) < (iv) < (i) < (iii)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "(i) $4 p$\n(ii) $4 \\mathrm{~s}$\n(iii) $3 d$\n(iv) $3 p$\nAccording to Bohr Bury's $(n+\\ell)$ rule, increasing order of energy will be (iv)  < (ii) $ < $ (iii) $ < $ (i).\nNote: If the two orbitals have same value of $(n+\\ell)$ then the orbital with lower value of $n$ will be filled first.",
    "image": null
